const PART: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "cm:2": [
    "Duala grew up beside the Wouri River, which is wide, muddy and full of boats. Long ago, Portuguese sailors saw so many prawns there, they named the land after the creature. Camarões, in Portuguese, sounds a little like the word for prawns. Estelle likes this part, because map and legend touch. The whole country fits inside one small river animal. The Duala people, though, add another owner to the water. They call her Mami Wata, the mother of the water. In this tale she is not a fright. She is the lady of the estuary, holding a small mirror. It shows her whether people treat the river like home.",
    "For one week, the port dropped far too much rubbish. The roar of engines drowned out the sound of your own oar. The nets came up almost empty, and the water smelled bad beside the pillars. An aunt of Estelle still greeted the river every morning. She said Mami Wata had not moved to the cool of any office. She had stayed in the muddy water, and she asked for respect. Not with film storms, but with fish that go missing and a bank that stops belonging to anyone. The city had grown on top of the myth, and the myth had not resigned.",
    "Estelle and her aunt went down at dawn with a basket. They did not take expensive offerings. They took simple flowers, a short song, and a bag for the plastic on the stakes. The aunt spoke to the water the way you speak to a neighbour. She said sorry for the noise and the rubbish, and promised not to use the river as a bin. Estelle repeated the name of the prawns and the name of Mami Wata. Both had to fit in the same sentence. The mirror of the legend is for the city to see itself. If the picture looks dirty, the mirror is not what is wrong.",
    "In the days that followed, the aunt's net came back with small fish and a few prawns. Enough for a sauce, not for a party. Nobody claimed to have seen a lady walking on the water. The lesson did not need an appearance. It needed a rule. The Duala know the laws of the estuary: say hello, do not make it dirty, do not take everything. The Wouri, tired of traffic, is still the owner. The port, the fish and the spirit live at the same address. She understood that her country's name was born in this river. Such a name is not treated with scorn.",
    "When she tells the story at school, Estelle joins the two halves together. The Portuguese saw the prawns, and the map kept that echo. Mami Wata, mirror in hand, asks that the echo stay alive. Without respect, the estuary is just a tired channel. With respect, Duala still has a lady in the muddy water. If you go to the Wouri, throw nothing into the current and leave some fish for tomorrow. The enchanted river, in this tale, is charmed by care. The rest is rubbish, and rubbish is not part of the legend.",
  ],
  "cm:3": [
    "In the south of Cameroon, the forest is tall, wet and full of voices. The trickster there is not always the same creature. It can be the tortoise, the palm rat or the spider. The spider, a cousin of Anansi, is not the only owner of the region, and the tale says so honestly. One afternoon, Estelle heard the version about the web. A young hunter lost his way in the rain. His footprints filled up with water. Then a spider came down to the level of his eyes. It offered a bright thread, stretched from branch to branch, like a low lantern.",
    "‘Follow the web to the edge of the forest,’ said the spider. ‘One condition only: do not boast. The forest lends its path to those who do not think they own it.’ The hunter swore. He walked in silence, his hand light on the thread, and the rain seemed less heavy. From far off he saw the calm shape of a gorilla. He did not shout, point or run. The web led him to where the trees opened onto the village garden. He was safe, wet and whole. So far he had kept his promise. Pride waited for him at the very last step, which is where it usually waits.",
    "As soon as he saw people, the hunter raised his arms and shouted that the forest was his. He said he had never been lost, and that even the gorillas moved aside for him. The web snapped like an old thread and disappeared. The path closed up with leaves. Night fell early, the way night falls under the canopy. He sat on a root with no swagger left, and he heard the gorillas far away. Deep voices, with no attack and no welcome. He spent the night learning how big a man on foot really is. Humility, in that jungle, was not decoration. It was the map.",
    "In the morning, hoarse from so much silence, he whispered a thank you with no audience. A thin thread began to shine again on the first tree, and it took him home by a short way. The spider did not give a lecture. The silence of the night had been the lecture. Estelle, listening to the story with rain on the roof, laughed. She understood that the forest at night is a school. Anansi, or his southern cousin, charges for pride with a lesson, not with a cruel trap. Without the web, the hero is only someone lost. With the web and silence, he arrives.",
    "The people of southern Cameroon tell this with wet feet. If the forest helps you, do not turn the help into a speech. The gorillas were not there to applaud you. The spider does not lend light to anyone who wears it like a crown. The child repeats the tale and keeps the message in a pocket. Ask, say thank you, and leave boasting for a day that never comes. That way the web stays whole and the night stays only night. The way home does not fade mid-sentence.",
  ],
  "cm:4": [
    "In Yaoundé, the city of hills, there is a parrot that children call by name. Yet every yard claims it is theirs. This tale is recent and urban, and it is honest to say so. The real forest parrot was copying voices long before a capital existed. The city legend only dressed it in an imaginary jacket and sat it down to listen to the square. The bird was grey and alert. Estelle passed the market as it repeated the greeting of three different sellers. It kept every accent exactly. The street laughed. The parrot gave a crooked bow.",
    "There was also a man who talked big. He made loud promises and repeated one sentence until it shone. In the square, he explained how he would run the district and the building work. He would even run the rain, if asked. He spoke so fast that his words tripped over each other. The parrot, on a window railing, listened with its head tilted, like a very good pupil. Nobody had given it a speech to learn. It chose by itself. Cameroon holds more than two hundred languages, and a bird that copies is understood in all of them. It gives back the sound exactly as it received it.",
    "The next day, the man came back for another speech. He had barely opened his mouth when the parrot started first, with the same pompous voice and the same promises. It even had the same little cough. The square laughed kindly, not cruelly. The man heard himself from the outside, like someone seeing himself in an unexpected mirror, and he went red. Every big sentence, said by the bird, seemed emptier. He closed his coat, which in this tale is the jacket of the legend, and sat down. The parrot fell silent the moment the man fell silent. There was nothing left to give back.",
    "Estelle came closer and gave the bird a piece of fruit, not a secret. Her grandmother, who was with her, said the forest already knew this joker. The city had simply found him an audience of officials, sellers and neighbours. Anyone who talks too much risks hearing the speech again, in a public square, with feathers on. It is not a heavy spell. It is a mirror with a beak. Measuring your tongue, in Yaoundé as in the village, is still good manners. That afternoon, the man bought mangoes and asked the price in a quiet voice. The street liked him better that way.",
    "Living folklore is born like this, on top of an old bird and a noisy capital. Estelle tells the Yaoundé parrot to her cousins. She laughs when one of them copies an adult for no reason. The lesson fits inside the beak: words come back. If they are not fair, they come back laughing. If they are simple, the parrot only says good morning, and that is enough. The forest lent the mimic. The city lent the jacket. If you have a big sentence on the tip of your tongue, try saying it quietly first. Your own yard may already be listening.",
  ],
  "au:0": [
    "In Australia, many Aboriginal peoples tell how the kangaroo got its pouch, and not every version is the same. It is fair to say so, because each people keeps its own law in the Time of the Dreaming. In the red desert of the interior, the story begins with a mother kangaroo and her small young. On the coast, the story is about a creator who thinks of dry journeys. Both should be heard as living law, not as fairy tales.",
    "In one version, the mother kangaroo ran through the dry scrub because an angry animal was chasing her. There was no burrow nearby, and her children were too small to hop alone all day. She pressed her young against her belly and asked the earth for help. Her skin made a deep fold, like a warm pocket. The little ones hid inside, and the animal passed without seeing them. The mother said: ‘Stay quiet, and I will carry you with me.’",
    "In another version, the creator looked at the dry land of Australia. He saw long journeys with no waterhole in sight. The joey, which is a baby kangaroo, could not bear the sun alone on the hot desert ground. So the creator gave the mother a pouch on her belly. The creator said: ‘Carry your child with you. You never leave it behind.’ The mother said thank you and went on along the dry path. The joey was safe even when the water was far away.",
    "From that day on, the joey travels on its mother's body with its nose out. It looks at the world without falling. In the desert and on the coast, the kangaroo is not a postcard toy. It is family, with a place in the old stories. The pouch was born from a sacred need, not from a game. If you see a kangaroo in Australia, remember this: the mother carries her young because the land asked her to.",
    "In this great land, the mother carries the world with her. She never leaves her child in the sun while she looks for food. Aboriginal peoples teach that telling the story is a way of caring for the law. It is not about laughing at the animal as if it wore a mask. The pouch is a home that walks, warm and close to the heart. It goes from the red desert to the blue sea. If you respect the tale, you treat the kangaroo as an old neighbour. It is not a funny drawing on a jumper.",
  ],
  "au:1": [
    "In Gippsland, in the south of Australia, Aboriginal peoples tell of Tiddalik, an enormous and very thirsty frog. One day his thirst grew bigger than any well, river or billabong in that green land. He drank the creek, then the river, and he did not stop until there was no water left anywhere around. The fish were stranded in the mud and the trees dropped their leaves. This tale is not just a joke for you to laugh at.",
    "The animals looked at the cracked earth and went to speak to Tiddalik. His belly looked like a round, heavy mountain. The eel twisted in front of him and said: ‘You have drunk our water. Give it back, or we all dry up in this sun.’ Tiddalik did not want to open his mouth. He was full, stubborn and very pleased with himself. Then someone remembered a simple idea. If the frog really laughed, the water might flow back into the rivers.",
    "The eel danced on the dry mud, twisting its body like a ribbon. The other animals tried songs, jumps and funny faces. They failed, and Tiddalik only blinked, very serious, with his belly shining in the hot Gippsland sun. The eel did not give up. She danced so crookedly that even the tortoise lost its balance and rolled over. Then Tiddalik let out an enormous laugh and his mouth opened wide. The water rushed out in one great burst.",
    "The water came back, but it came back far too fast. It flooded the valleys, the roots and the smooth stones. The rivers filled, the billabongs returned, and the fish found their way home again. The animals climbed to high places until the land drank what was extra and grew calm again. Tiddalik became smaller and tired, and the eel said: ‘One gulp is enough, because the world is not your cup.’",
    "Australia of the drought understands Tiddalik with no effort. You can laugh at the frog and still hear the thirst. The legend warns anyone who wants to drink all the water alone. The rivers are not a private cup. If you drink the world, the world asks you to laugh. Your belly cannot hold what you kept by force. In Gippsland this is still told with care, because the rivers in this story are one enormous hiccup.",
  ],
  "au:2": [
    "The Rainbow Serpent is an ancestral being of many Aboriginal peoples of Australia, and not one closed story. It is a whole family of laws, and it is right to say so with respect. We should not squeeze all peoples into one picture. She carves rivers with her body, rises when the rain calls, and guards the waterholes where people drink. The colours of the rainbow are her body travelling between water and sky. Wherever she passed, the water became sacred.",
    "In the Dreaming, the land of Australia was flat, hot and dry. There was no way for the rain to come down and stay. The Rainbow Serpent moved forward slowly, and every curve of her body opened a new river bed. Waterholes were born at the bends, and the animals followed the cool track to drink with care. She warned in a clear voice: ‘Do not dirty the waterhole, because this water is a home. You must treat it like one.’",
    "When someone dirties a waterhole, the Serpent does not shout like a film monster, only to scare small children. The water closes up, the fish vanish, and thirst teaches the lesson without blood and without shouting on the bank. She punishes anyone who treats the waterhole as a thing with no owner. The law belongs to whoever made the river. If you clean what you made dirty and you ask permission, the waterhole can open again for everyone to drink.",
    "Places with water are sacred because she passed through them. An Aboriginal child learns the name of the waterhole early. If you visit those lands, you are not the owner of the map. You are a guest, and your step should be short and humble. Do not draw the Serpent as a mascot on a jumper, because she is the living map of Australia itself. Without her the desert has no river, and with her every waterhole has a story, a rule and a memory.",
    "After the rain, the rainbow opens over Australia, and the old people know who is looking down from above. The Rainbow Serpent travels between the sky and the water, and her body is made of colour after the storm. The law is simple, even for you. Do not dirty the waterhole, do not empty the river and do not laugh at what is sacred. If you take care of the water, the ancestor leaves the path open. If you do not, thirst closes the land again.",
  ],
  "au:3": [
    "Bunjil the eagle is the creator and ancestor of the Koolin and Wathaurong peoples of southern Australia. He is not the ancestor of every people at once. Telling Bunjil means naming those peoples, not just saying ‘the Aborigines’ as if they were one single group. Bunjil shaped people from clay, breathed life into them, and taught the laws for living together without greed. His brother, sometimes a darker bird, made the other side of the law. Together they left the world fit to live in.",
    "The clay was soft beside the rivers of the south, and Bunjil shaped each person's hands, feet and eyes. He said to each one: ‘You will take care of the land. You will not take more than your home needs.’ The dark brother taught the limit. He taught what must not be crossed, and what must not be broken for fun. Without both, the law was lame. With both, there was care on one side and a boundary on the other.",
    "When the work was done, Bunjil went up into the sky and stayed there as a star. He watches the south on clear nights. He did not abandon the people. He only changed his place, so that he could be seen from far away when the ground shows no footprints. Children learn to look for him in the dark. The elders say: ‘If you get lost, remember who made you from clay.’ The eagle needs no cage. The wide sky is its home.",
    "Melbourne stands on Bunjil's land, under this same sky, long before there were roads, lights and new names. The wedge-tailed eagle still rises in circles over the rivers and plains of Victoria. You can see one and hear the old name, without pretending the city erased the ancestor who came first. In this story, the law came from wings and the clay came from hands. The sky was the road home.",
    "The eagle created, taught and rose up. His brother held the other side of the law, so the world would not tilt. The people of clay still owe care to the river, the animal and the ground that holds them up. If you live in Melbourne, or pass through it, remember who was here before the squares and the trains. It is not a colouring picture to finish in a hurry. It is an ancestor, and at night the star still asks whether the law is standing.",
  ],
  "au:4": [
    "Australia has kept stories for sixty-five thousand years. They do not fit on one page or belong to one people. Elsewhere there are also stories of stolen fire and of the first death. The Rainbow Serpent's law changes with the land. Here we choose a version from the north coast: the serpent of the waters meeting the great sea. Where the river reaches the ocean, she lies down, and the white surf is her slow breathing.",
    "The peoples of the north coast know these river mouths as places of law. They are not a postcard for a quick visit. This is where the body of an ancestor breathes between fresh water and salt water, in one place. Before fishing, people ask permission, because a line alone is not enough and the fish have a home. The elders say: ‘You do not walk into someone's house shouting, and here the house is the water.’",
    "On the north coast, a hurried fisherman threw his net into the river mouth without a word of respect. The wind changed, the net came back empty, and the foam rose to his knees like a calm warning. He sat on the sand, asked permission in a low voice, and thanked the river and the sea. Only then did the water grow calm. He took only the fish his pot at home needed, not one more.",
    "The coast is not the end of the map. It is the place where the ancestor lies down and stays alive in the wave. On the beach, you can learn to go into the water like a guest entering a house. Bring permission, care and thanks. Do not throw rubbish into the river mouth, and do not shout just to prove you are brave. The surf you hear is breathing, not empty noise, and the peoples of the north coast already knew this.",
    "There are stories of fire and of the first death, and also this one. It is faithful to the water that meets the salt. The Rainbow Serpent lies at the bar, and the wave rises and falls with her chest, with no hurry. The message fits in your open hand: ask permission before you take what the tide shows you. If you respect the river mouth, fishing is a visit. If you do not, the house closes again.",
  ],
  "th:0": [
    "In Thailand, the pale elephant is real and sacred, and it belongs to the king. That whiteness is a sign of merit, the worth of someone who must be fair. It is not a circus clown or a party toy. In processions and temples you see it like a living throne. The stories of Siddhattha, who became the Buddha, mix with the jataka tales. In those, he lived lives as a wise elephant. Here, the white is a duty, not decoration to point at and then forget.",
    "Before Siddhattha was born, his mother dreamed of a white elephant. The people read the dream as a good sign. In the jataka tales, the wise elephant shares out water and shade. He does not keep the well for his own herd. In Thailand, these stories met the law of the kingdom: whoever receives a pale elephant also receives an examination. Merit is not a crown to shine on its own. It is a promise to care for anyone who is thirsty and hot.",
    "There was a king who put a white elephant to work without rest, in the strong sun. Meanwhile his people waited for water in the dry fields. The elephant refused. It did not move forward, it did not lower its head and it did not obey the stick. It stood still as a temple in the middle of the yard. The guards grew angry, but the animal did not stir. Not until the king promised, aloud, shade and water for the people too. Only then did it kneel, and the gesture said: ‘You rule if you give shade. If you do not, I will not serve you.’",
    "The king kept his promise. He ordered water let into the fields. He had trees planted along the hot roads, where people walked with no shelter. The elephant returned to fair work, not cruel work, and the kingdom breathed more easily that long summer. In Thailand, a kingdom is measured by animals like this, not by gold alone. It is measured by how the strongest treats the most tired. If the merit is false, the elephant does not kneel. In the temple, a child learns to see the difference between a trick and a sign.",
    "Siddhattha, the jataka tales and this stubborn elephant tell the same lesson, each through its own door. Whoever holds the white must rule like someone who gives shade. Without that shade the title is empty, however high the throne may be. You can remember this far from Thailand. Power that does not share out water does not deserve anyone kneeling before it. In this story, the elephant judges the king, and a wise king thanks the judgement and changes his day.",
  ],
  "th:1": [
    "Nang Nak is one of the most famous legends of Thailand. It is a story of love and of goodbye, not an empty scare for the night. Nak lived in Phra Khanong, which is now part of Bangkok, with her husband Mak. She waited for him for a long time by the door of their house. She had a baby and became so ill that she died, and her body was taken away as custom demanded. Love, though, did not know how to leave. It stayed in the house, tending the lamp, as if life went on as before.",
    "When Mak came home, Nak welcomed him with the baby in her arms and hot soup. He saw no sign of goodbye anywhere. The days felt normal, with the sleeping mat and the lamp. Her voice said, ‘You're back, sit down, the house waited for you.’ The neighbours knew that Nak had already died. They were afraid to speak in front of Mak. At last, a neighbour held his arm by the market. She told him the truth in a low voice, with pity and without laughter.",
    "Mak did not want to believe it. He went home, looked at Nak, and noticed that the lamp cast no shadow at her feet. There was no shouting and no chase through the street. There was cold in his chest and a lot of sadness on both sides of the mat. Nak cried too, because the secret was over. She had not wanted to trap her husband. She only wanted not to be alone in the dark house. She said: ‘I waited, and I did not know how to leave.’ Mak held her hand. He did not pretend nothing had happened.",
    "A monk helped them both to say goodbye, not with anger but with kindness. It was like setting a bird free. In the place where the Mahabut temple now stands in Bangkok, people still remember this calm goodbye. Nak heard it and Mak heard it. The baby stayed with the living, with a name, food and arms to hold him. Nak stopped being the prison of the house. She became the protector of anyone who asks with respect and does not play with the pain of others.",
    "Thailand does not hide Nang Nak. It tells her story carefully and faithfully, because it is love and goodbye, not a game of scaring people. Without the goodbye, the house cannot breathe. With the goodbye, love changes shape and stops frightening the street and the neighbours. At the Mahabut temple, people still bring offerings to Nak: flowers, a request or a quiet thank you. If you enter this story, enter slowly, because it is sad and kind. Here, the goodbye is the act that saves them both.",
  ],
  "th:2": [
    "Phra Aphai Mani is a prince from a long poem by Sunthorn Phu. Thailand has taken him as her own story, told even to children. He does not win with the sword alone. He carries a staff and a flute, and each one serves a different door. The staff opens a way through the stones. The flute calms wild beasts, soldiers and even the angry waves. His father was angry with a son who studied music instead of war. Aphai left, and his brother went after him so he would not be lost.",
    "In the Gulf of Thailand, a sea giantess heard the flute and rose from a cave. She wanted him on her island. She carried him off, rolled a stone across the entrance, and did him no harm. She also did not let him leave to see the daylight. Aphai was not perfect. He was afraid and he was rushed. On some days he only stared at the gap, with no plan at all. The staff did not move the stone and the sword stuck fast. The music had not yet found the right place.",
    "A mermaid of the gulf liked Aphai and did not want to see him trapped. She was a firm friend, the kind who hears the flute and chooses to help. At high tide she moved the stone. She said: ‘Come now, and do not look back.’ His brother had been following the trail along the coast. He saw the signal and ran to meet them on the wet sand. The escape was tiring and real. The giantess shouted from deep down, but the sea was already carrying the small boat far away.",
    "On the next island the giantess caught up with them again. The staff held the passage for one moment only. It was long enough for Aphai to lift the flute to his lips. The music hurt nobody. It put the fury to sleep and lowered the waves. It made a way where the sword could not fit or help. Sunthorn Phu left this message in the middle of the adventure. Art can open what the sword jams shut, if you find the right note. Aphai was not perfect, and still he arrived.",
    "Children in Thailand know these scenes by heart. There is the soothing flute, the island and the escape over green water. The poem is long and full of sea, like the gulf. It passed from book into folklore told outside the pages. The giantess, the mermaid and the brother are not decorations. They are life in excess around a prince who makes mistakes and carries on. If you ever hear a flute near the water, remember Phra Aphai Mani. The way out is not always a blow. Sometimes it is a note at the right time.",
  ],
  "th:3": [
    "In the Ramakien, the Thai story that grew from the Ramayana, Hanuman is the white monkey. He is loyal, playful and very strong. He serves Phra Ram, the just prince. Nang Sida, the princess, was taken far away to the city of Lanka. The murals of Wat Phra Kaew in Bangkok tell this in metres of gold, so that nobody forgets the journey. People recognise Hanuman by the white mask in the khon, which is the classical dance. In Thailand he has even more humour and more strength than in other versions.",
    "Hanuman does not sit still in the palace, waiting for small orders. He leaps, he flies, and he crosses the sea as if the wind were a staircase. He jokes, pulls faces and gets into trouble, and even so he never forgets the right side or changes sides. Phra Ram asks him: ‘Go and bring me news of Nang Sida. Then come back.’ Hanuman strikes his chest and sets off, without boasting too much. He is not a clown without law. His humour fits inside his loyalty, and without that loyalty strength would only make a mess.",
    "In Lanka, Hanuman searches for Nang Sida. He tells her that Phra Ram has not forgotten her, even with the sea between them. The guards try to catch him, and he escapes with leaps and tricks. Fire takes hold of the roofs of the city that holds her. The murals show the flames and also the care. The princess is not left in the fire and not abandoned behind. Hanuman flies back with the message delivered. Bangkok paints this return in gold to say that good did not arrive quietly.",
    "In the khon dance, Hanuman's white mask moves quickly. Children laugh before they understand the whole journey and the whole duty. Laughter is part of the epic. It is not an extra, and the Ramakien is not a copy left on another country's shelf. It is the story made Thai, with Phra Ram, Nang Sida and the monkey who can become the wind. You see the mask and you know the side. He serves, he plays, and he does not change sides when the leap gets hard.",
    "Without Hanuman, Nang Sida does not come back. With him, the enemy palace burns, and good still dances at the end of the road. It is not a lesson in burning for fun. It is the lesson of a friend who crosses the sea when others only watch from the bank. In Wat Phra Kaew, the gold still tells of the leap, the fire and the return, without hiding the effort. If you stay on Phra Ram's side, as Hanuman does, strength gains a name and a duty. The white monkey tips his head, smiles, and the way is made.",
  ],
  "th:4": [
    "The naga is the serpent of the waters, ancestor of the Mekong River and of the temples of Thailand. On the stairways of the wats, which are temples, its stone body makes a handrail. It is not there just for decoration. It is family, and the princess of the land loved a naga of the water. These worlds do not join without a rite. If the river is insulted, it rises. If it is honoured, it leaves the stairway in peace. The princess went down to the Mekong at the end of the day. She spoke to him without fear and without scorn.",
    "The naga was huge, with scales like wet leaves. He did not ask the princess to leave the people on the bank. He said: ‘You live on the bank and I live in the depths. The rite is the bridge between our two houses.’ The elders taught the words, the offering and the right day. They took their time, and did not turn it into a wedding of one short moment. It was not a careless promise. It was an agreement so that the flood would not take the rice or the village door. Anyone who kept the rite slept with the river in its place.",
    "One year, someone laughed at the naga and threw rubbish into the river. They said temple stone does not bite or care. The water rose in the night and licked at the doors. The new rice was in danger at the edge of the fields. The princess went down barefoot, said sorry on behalf of the bank, and performed the rite they had deliberately forgotten. The naga grew calm and the flood drew back to the old line. He said: ‘I do not charge fear, I charge respect. You have brought it.’",
    "In the real Mekong, there are still nights of lights over the water. They are called bang fai phaya nak, and people say the nagas are celebrating. Science speaks of gases, the people speak of sacred serpents, and you do not need to shout at either side. In the temple, a child climbs between nagas and holds the stone handrail like the hand of an old relative. The legend is that handrail. It leads you to the Buddha without slipping, if your foot is humble and the river is clean.",
    "The princess and the naga remain as a warning and as a bridge. Different worlds can meet without scorn. They ask for a rite, not a laugh thrown into the water. Without respect for the river, the naga charges with a flood that licks at the doors. With respect, the stairway leads to the temple and the rice stays in the field, dry and whole. In Thailand, people on the Mekong light a candle. The river decides whether the light under the water is gas or family. If you go, walk slowly and do not dirty the step.",
  ],
  "id:4": [
    "On the island of Komodo, in Indonesia, the ground is dry and the hills are low. The sun lies heavy on the stones. A real dragon lives there, and it is not made of smoke. It is the giant monitor lizard, which the islanders call the ora. It walks slowly, on strong legs, and it knows every shadow in the grass. Biology says this animal is unlike any other in the world. The island legend says something else beside that truth. The two fit in the same landscape without pushing each other out.",
    "The Ata Modo, the people of Komodo, tell that a mother had twins. One was a boy, with a voice and hands, and the other was a dragon, with a tail and silence. They came from the same lap and cried on the same day. So they are not hunter and prey. They are family, and the boy grew up and became a person of the village. The ora went to live in the dry hills. When they meet on the path, the story asks that neither pretend not to know a brother.",
    "Because of that birth, nobody kills a dragon for nothing. Not for jokes, and not to boast by a fire. The island, even when it is harsh and has no big rivers, treats the creature as kin. There are rules for passing, for distance and for respect. If the ora comes along the trail, a person steps aside. It is not only fear. It is old manners, like when a grandfather enters the room and the talk goes quiet without anyone asking.",
    "A grandmother in the village of Komodo takes her grandchildren to the path when the light is still soft. If an ora crosses, she says quietly, ‘You stop, you speak softly and you let him pass. He is your brother.’ The children step onto the verge with quiet feet. The dragon goes on into the grass with no hurry. Nobody throws a stone, and nobody runs about making noise. The agreement shows best in those small minutes, better than in any long speech about the island.",
    "Without the word brother, the monitor lizard would just be a creature you run from or boast about. With it, the whole of Komodo is an agreement between two twins. It is not a park full of monsters, but a shared cradle: dry, real and old. If you go to the island, go as a family visitor, not as the owner of a rare animal. The ora was already there when the legend learned to say mother. Respect is the part of the story that can still be kept today.",
  ],
  "vn:0": [
    "In the time of the Hùng kings, in the north of Vietnam, the king had a daughter. Princess Mỵ Nương was known for her calm and her beauty. Two spirits asked for her hand. Sơn Tinh was the spirit of Mount Tản Viên, lord of the slopes and the stones. Thủy Tinh was the spirit of the water, lord of the rivers and the sea. Both came to the palace in a hurry to marry. The king did not want to choose by the loudest shout. He wanted a test that everyone could see.",
    "The king said he would marry his daughter to whoever brought the wedding gifts first. Sơn Tinh did not waste the morning. He brought gifts from the land: jade, rare fruits from the hillside and gentle animals from the mountain. He placed them all before the king before the light changed. Mỵ Nương went with him to Tản Viên. She had already chosen, because he arrived in time and the mountain offered a firm home. The palace closed that door with music, and the other suitor was still on the road.",
    "Thủy Tinh arrived later, with gifts from the sea: pearls and salt water in bowls. He found the feast already over. His anger was huge. From then on, every year, he calls the rivers and the rain and rises to attack the mountain. He tries to take what he did not win. The Red River swells, the banks tremble, and the villages of the delta see the water climbing the steps. Sơn Tinh does not run away. He raises the land, lifts hills and holds the dykes, so the princess's home stays above the flood.",
    "When the river rises, a child in a delta village hears the grandmother at the window say both names. The princess in the middle of the legend is not undecided. She already lives on the mountain, and the sea is the one who refuses the answer. The war comes back with the rainy season, without needing a field full of soldiers. It is weather with jealousy. People carry their belongings up to the attic and say a little prayer. They trust the land will rise in time once more.",
    "Without Sơn Tinh, the village would sink. Without Thủy Tinh, the rice would dry out and the delta would forget good thirst. Strange as it seems, the struggle also brings the water that feeds the fields, if the dykes hold. The Vietnam of the rivers lives inside this old dispute. If you see a flood in the news, remember Mỵ Nương on the mountain and the two spirits. One won the wedding, and the other never stopped knocking at the door with the whole river.",
  ],
  "vn:1": [
    "In the heart of Hanoi there is a calm lake called Hoàn Kiếm, with a small tower and light boats. In old times, Lê Lợi was fighting to free the land from the Ming armies. The war was heavy for the people. A fisherman called Lê Thận pulled a magic sword out of the water. It was bright without being cruel, and the blade reached Lê Lợi. It was not a weapon to keep in a room of vanity. It was a loan from the Dragon King, Long Vương, made for a hard time.",
    "With the sword, Lê Lợi gave his people courage and opened paths, and at last the war ended. Peace returned to the streets, the market opened again, and people could say each other's names without haste to flee. He could have hung the blade on the wall for everyone to admire. He did not. Deep down, he knew that borrowed power starts to lie. It lies when peace has come and the sword stays out of its house. The lake waited, green, in the middle of a city breathing again.",
    "Lê Lợi went out in a small boat on Hoàn Kiếm. The water opened, and a golden turtle rose: the messenger Kim Quy, with a shell shining like an old coin. The turtle asked for the sword back, with firm politeness, so it could return it to the Dragon King. The emperor did not argue. He drew the blade, gave it up, and the turtle sank with it to the bottom. There was no fight. There was an act of giving back. From that moment, the lake had a new name and a memory in the mud.",
    "Hoàn Kiếm means the return of the sword. Hanoi treats the lake as an altar, not as any old tank of water. For many years a huge turtle really did live there. The city came to see it as if the legend stood on the bank. Children feed the fish and hear the story while sitting on the stone. Nobody asks for the sword again, just to play with it. It is where it should be when peace keeps the house safe.",
    "The legend asks for something simple and hard. The weapon goes back to the water when it is no longer needed. In this tale, power is borrowed, and whoever forgets that carries a weight that is not his. If you hold something strong that was given to help, ask yourself if it is time to give it back. The turtle delivered the message, and the sword under the lake hopes it will not be needed. Hanoi, with the lake in its heart, remembers this every day.",
  ],
  "vn:2": [
    "At the festival of Tết, the Vietnamese New Year, kitchens fill with banana leaves and boiling rice. The story begins in the time of the Hùng king. The old capital was Phong Châu, in the region of Phú Thọ. The king wanted to choose the son who would rule after him. He asked each one for a gift that showed love for the land, not just riches for the palace. The princes set off on long journeys. One of them, Lang Liêu, was poor in gold and rich in care for the fields.",
    "The brothers came back with ivory, jewels and boxes that shone even in the shade. Lang Liêu had none of it, and he hardly dared to enter the hall. One night he dreamed a simple piece of advice. He should cook what the ground gives, with the care of a son, and offer that to his father. He woke before sunrise and called those who knew how to light the fire. He went out to the garden and the rice field. There was no rush to the market. There was a rush to do a modest thing well.",
    "He made a square cake of rice and beans, wrapped in leaves, and called it bánh chưng. The square was the Earth, firm, with corners that could sit on a table. He also made a round cake, bánh giầy, smooth and pale, which was the Sky. He did not bring precious stones. He brought the taste of the fields and salt. He brought beans, and the patience to wrap the leaf without tearing it. When he set them before the king, some brothers smiled, because their gold made more noise.",
    "The Hùng king tasted the cakes, said nothing for a moment, and saw further than the shining boxes. He said the gift of the ground was worth more than the treasure of the palace. A people eats earth and sky made into rice, not gold. He chose Lang Liêu. The brothers with the jewels stayed at the edge of the story, forgotten little by little. The son who honoured the garden stayed at the centre of memory. That day, Phong Châu learned to measure a kingdom on another scale.",
    "Every Tết, Vietnam cooks this legend, and the house smells of warm leaves. If you help to wrap the cake, you are a little bit Lang Liêu, even far from Phú Thọ. The story does not change: whoever honours the land wins the throne of memory. Whoever brings only gold is forgotten when real hunger comes. Without rice, there is no sky or earth to share at the table. In this kitchen, the whole of the folklore fits in your hands.",
  ],
  "vn:3": [
    "Thạch Sanh was an orphan boy who lived in a hut at the edge of a forest in Vietnam. He had no parents in the doorway and no party clothes. But his hands were clean and he did not lie to seem bigger. He shared his little rice with anyone who passed. The whole village knew his name and his door that was always ajar. He did not ask for a palace. He asked that the path to the spring be safe for the children of the morning. That already filled his heart.",
    "In that forest there was a huge serpent, hidden among roots, that frightened travellers. One day it almost caught him as he went to fetch firewood. Thạch Sanh did not stay hidden in the hut for ever. He went back, faced the serpent with quiet courage and beat it, until the trail fell silent once more. From that trial he received a magic guitar, and it was not a fairground toy. It was an instrument that, when played, told the truth that important mouths like to cover up.",
    "At the palace, a lying mandarin, a high official, told the king that the brave deeds had been his. He wore silk, spoke loudly and pointed at the wood like a farm owner. Thạch Sanh, the orphan who had really been there, was pushed aside, because his clothes did not shine. Almost nobody in the hall wanted to hear a boy with no family. The mandarin smiled, and the lie sat in a better chair than the truth. And the guitar had not yet played.",
    "They let Thạch Sanh keep the instrument, perhaps out of contempt. When he played, the music entered the hall without asking permission and carried the true story to the king. The notes told of the forest, the serpent and the hut. The mandarin's lie fell, not to a sword, but to a sound that could not decorate a falsehood. The king understood who had cleared the path. The powerful man in silk grew small, and the orphan, standing, still had clean hands.",
    "Thạch Sanh married the princess, and that marriage was a fair ending, not a prize torn away by force. Vietnam loves this tale the way it loves a decent boy against a false powerful man. Without the guitar, the truth does not enter the palace. With it, the mandarin falls. If you are small and someone big tells your story backwards, do not dirty it to win. Play it straight, like Thạch Sanh. The forest and the court are one world when hands do not lie.",
  ],
  "vn:4": [
    "In the north of Vietnam, Hạ Long Bay opens in Quảng Ninh like a green dream. It is full of limestone islands as tall as towers. The name means the dragon that comes down. The water is calm on good days, and the boats look like ants among the stones. The landscape is so strange that a legend here seems like a report. If you go by boat, the first thing you do is point. Every island asks for a name and a story at once.",
    "The people tell that when invaders came by sea, dragons came down from the sky to defend the coast. They spat jewels into the water, not to decorate a king, but to stop the ships. Those jewels hardened and became islands. Every islet is a tooth of defence, set in the path of anyone coming to do harm. The old battle ended, and the sea stayed green and open to boats. The dragons are not in sight. But the whole bay proves they passed through with their mouths full of precious stone.",
    "There is another story for the same skyline. A pair of giants, tired of walking, sat down to rest and look at the green sea. They sat so still, side by side, that time turned them into stone. There was no shout. There was a rest that forgot to end. Since then they look out over the bay, huge and patient, without needing to move to stay together. The boatmen know their outline in the line of rocks. They lower their voices, as if passing in front of someone asleep.",
    "A girl called Lan, sitting in a small boat, tugs her mother's sleeve. She asks which island is the giant couple. Her mother points to a double shape far away and says, ‘There they are, looking at the water. The rest of the bay came down with the dragon, jewel after jewel.’ Lan is not afraid. She waves, because a statue that watches the sea also knows how to receive a greeting. The boat moves between the cool shadows of the stones. The limestone drips with light, and from here the legend does not seem a lie. It seems like the map.",
    "Without the myth, Hạ Long would be just a very beautiful place on a list. With it, every island keeps a gesture of protection. The stone couple reminds us that even giants stop to look. In this story, the sea was a field of gods, and now it is green. The dragon sleeps enough for the boats to pass. If you go, do not make the noise of an owner. Greet the couple, let the dragon rest, and take home the green, not a stone that is not yours.",
  ],
  "sa:0": [
    "On desert nights, between Najd and Hejaz, the caravan goes wherever the sand allows. A small fire is lit and coffee is boiled with cardamom. The first sip, in a little cup, is offered to the guest. Only then does someone tell a story. Without that story, the road is just a tiring stretch. With it, the night gives company. Saudi Arabia inherits this old stage: the voice, the fire, the people sitting in a circle. A child, even one living in a city today, still hears a rule. Telling stories is hospitality, as serious as breaking bread.",
    "The story that comes back most often is that of Scheherazade. There was a wounded king with a hard heart, and in his house neither stories nor people lasted until morning. The end came too early inside those walls, and it was too cold. Scheherazade did not bring a sword. She asked permission to tell a tale. She spoke in a clear voice, with no haste for decoration. She spoke right up to the loveliest knot in the plot, then stopped. The king, who already had the final word in his mouth, wanted to know the rest. To hear the rest, he had to let the whole night pass.",
    "Dawn came, and the hurried punishment did not happen, because the story was unfinished. The next night Scheherazade went on. Again she stopped at the point where nobody can get up from the mat. So a thread of nights grew longer. The angry king was learning, with no lecture, that a voice can be stronger than a bad habit. Around the caravan fire, when this is told, nobody treats Scheherazade as a dressed-up doll. They treat her as proof. The right word, said in time, puts off a sad ending and holds a kingdom together through curiosity.",
    "A boy, leaning on his grandfather's knee, asked whether the sand listens too. His grandfather said yes, in the way of the tale. A message crosses the dunes because someone puts it inside a story. Someone else repeats it at the next oasis. The coffee goes from hand to hand. The guest drinks first. One big fear grows small when the next sentence has not yet been said. That is the fear of a king who let nothing last till morning. The boy understood that hospitality is not only a cup. It is not leaving the other person without the rest of what began.",
    "After many nights, the king's anger cooled, and the story could end in peace, not at the executioner's block. Scheherazade had lived by her voice, and the kingdom had lived with her. In the desert all around, the silence listens. If you are ever a guest on a night like this, accept the first sip. Then listen to the end, even if the end comes tomorrow. A caravan with no story is just trodden sand. A caravan with a story is a moving house. A voice well used is not decoration. It is what stops the dark from closing the talk too soon.",
  ],
  "sa:1": [
    "Desert coffee, the qahwa, is not just a drink. It is the law of the tent. The host serves and the guest receives. The little cup is not filled to the brim, because a full brim looks like vanity. It is drunk bitter, with cardamom, in silence enough for the other person to exist. In Saudi Arabia, a child learns to serve before learning to make speeches. Without qahwa, there are doors that do not open. With qahwa, even someone who arrived angry finds a place to rest his hands. The sweet comes afterwards, if it comes, and only if the talk deserves it.",
    "Where did the fruit that keeps us awake come from? One story travelled from Ethiopia with the shepherd Kaldi, who saw his goats grow lively after eating certain berries. Another story, told in the Arab lands, speaks of a dervish. He tasted the fruit and stayed awake for evening prayer, grateful and alert. Mecca and Yemen appear on the routes, and some argue about which path came first. We say this without pretending that one version erases the other. What matters in the tent is not winning the argument. It is not leaving the cup empty when your guest sits down.",
    "Faisal was old enough to start serving. On a hot afternoon, a neighbour who had argued the day before about a well came into the tent. He was stiff and unsmiling. Faisal's father did not begin with the well. He signalled to his son. Faisal took the coffee pot in both hands, as he had been taught. He served the little cup without filling it to the brim. The neighbour drank. He served his father, and only then took a tiny sip himself. Nobody had yet said the word sorry. The cardamom steam was already doing its slow work.",
    "The talk about the well came at last, in low voices, between one cup and the next. The neighbour explained his fear of being left without water. The father explained his fear of being pushed out of turn. They did not become brothers in song within a minute. They became people able to finish the sentence. Faisal understood that coffee did not solve the thirst of the whole desert. It solved the hurry to shout. Yesterday's enemy could speak, because the tent had opened by the old law, not by victory. Outside, the sun was still hard. Inside, the cup was small on purpose.",
    "When the neighbour got up, the father did not rush to offer sweet things. He waited. The talk had been fair, even though it was short, and then the sweet came, modest, with no big fuss. Faisal kept the lesson better than he kept the taste. The legend of the fruit that keeps us awake is the origin. The ritual is the gift. Arabia, in this tale, fits in one bitter cup: serve without pride, drink with no hurry, and speak only afterwards. If you offer something to someone, do not fill it until it spills, just to look generous. Leave space. Peace can sit down in that space.",
  ],
  "sa:2": [
    "A hunter lost his way in the interior of Arabia. He was on the dry paths that lead to the oases of Al-Ahsa and Tayma. The sun was a stone on top of his head. The water in his skin bag was gone. He no longer thought of glory. He thought of thirst, and thirst makes short ideas. Then he saw a gazelle. It was slender and quick, the colour of sand when sand wants to be light. The hunter raised his bow, because that was his tool and because hunger was speaking too. The gazelle did not freeze. It looked at him and took two steps to the side.",
    "He followed, with the arrow still ready, sure that she would tire first. The gazelle did not run like someone fleeing for ever. She ran a short way, stopped and looked back. Then she walked on towards a patch of green he had not seen. It looked like a mistake of the sun. The hunter, suspicious, thought of shooting right there, before the mirage carried her away. His hand shook with tiredness, not with kindness. Even so, he did not let go of the string. There was something about her that seemed like a guide, not prey. He took ten more steps, and the green stopped being a lie.",
    "It was an oasis, small and real, with dark water in the shade. The gazelle reached it and did not drink. She waited, trembling at the side, as if the law of the place depended on what the man did next. The hunter understood late, and just in time. He lowered the bow and left it on the sand, far from his hand. He did not drink first. The Bedouin story is clear: the first sip belongs to whoever showed the way. The gazelle drank. Only then did he kneel and drink too. He drank more slowly, ashamed that he had nearly traded a life for a shot.",
    "The legend says that from then on, this oasis refuses anyone who shoots first. If the hunter had let the arrow go, the water would have stayed hidden. He would have been bones on the sand. Killing the guide, when she is the only door, is the crime the tale does not forgive. He bowed his head, as if thanking a person. She looked at him for one more moment, whole and free. Then she vanished among the palm trees, with no fear in her hurry. The bow stayed on the ground until he remembered he was a man, not the owner of the world.",
    "He returned to the camp with less pride and with his life. He told the young ones that the gazelle is not just speed. It is grace that can be betrayed, if thirst rules over everything. The oases of Al-Ahsa and Tayma know this law. It is spoken in the shade of a tent, and not only on the bank. The interior of Arabia still measures a person by the thirst of another. If someone leads you to water, do not be the first to drink. Never raise your weapon against the guide. The oasis opens when the bow lies down.",
  ],
  "sa:3": [
    "In Jeddah, facing the Red Sea, a child asked the old storyteller whether the sea ever ended. The old man laughed quietly and said that for Sindbad, the sea always began again. Sindbad was a merchant, not a saint. He liked to come home rich, and sometimes he liked it too much. Even so, he came back to tell the tale. The Arabia of the coast hears these voyages like news of a cousin from the port. You do not need to move his birthplace for him to feel close. Just look at the water and admit that someone once said: listen.",
    "On one of the seven voyages, Sindbad fell into a valley of diamonds. It was beautiful and dangerous, with no way out on foot. Above it flew the Roc, a bird so big that its shadow blotted out the sun. Sindbad was afraid, and even with fear he thought. He hid and tied himself to a piece of meat. Merchants used such meat to lure the bird down. The Roc carried him out of the valley. He took a few stones, and his heart leapt. When his feet touched safe ground, he did not dance. He sat down and understood that being alive was the best goods.",
    "He went home proud, with the stones shining, and pride asked him for another voyage. More always seemed to be too little. He set out again, and the sea charged its price. A storm took the cargo and the wood groaned. He clung to a plank, poor once more, under a sky with no giant bird. There was no monster to eat him in that part of the story. There was water, thirst and time to think. Sindbad, who could make mistakes, saw that greed also weighs, as much as a sack of stones. The plank was not a throne. It was a small, wet second chance.",
    "On another voyage, an old man of the sea asked to ride on Sindbad's back. His tired voice made kind Sindbad agree. Up there, the old man would not climb down. There was no blow and no cruelty. He walked and thought, and offered fruit until the old man fell asleep, full at last. Then he set him down gently on the sand and was free. Here, cleverness does not humiliate anyone. It gets you out from under a weight. Sindbad went on until there was a return. A voyage is complete when someone hears the words I am back.",
    "He grew old in the port, with less hurry and seven voyages in his voice. The child of Jeddah does not need to swear she saw the Roc. She needs to believe in the return. The sea charges. The story pays. Seven times is enough for one man. The eighth voyage belongs to whoever stays at home, wiser, listening, without having to prove everything on his own skin. If you look at the Red Sea and want to chase every sparkle, remember Sindbad. Leave if you must. Come back for real. And tell it, or the water keeps only the price.",
  ],
  "sa:4": [
    "The Arabian horse, the asil, is not a fantasy creature. It is an old and real breed. It has a broad forehead, alert ears and a loyalty the desert learned to recognise. Some say it was born from the wind. Others remember, with respect, the mare of the Prophet. The story Saudi children hear most clearly is the one about the five mares. Without this horse, the long distances would have been a harsher and lonelier empire. In many tales, the Bedouin treats the horse as family. Sometimes the tent is just big enough for both of them.",
    "A man of the desert was riding far with his mares when real thirst arrived. It was not the thirst of one hour. It was the thirst that changes your pace and makes your mouth forget words. The mares held out with him, day after day, until at last water appeared, a low shine between the stones. Any animal would have run. The man felt his own throat burning too. Even so, he remembered an old test. It was not meant to be cruel. It was meant to show who would hear his voice once the water was calling louder than any owner.",
    "He moved away from the water and called. Most of the mares, beaten by thirst, ran to the shine and drank. Five stopped. They were as thirsty as the others, their flanks trembling. Even so, they turned to the voice and went to him before drinking. Only when he led them back to the bank, already chosen, did they drink. There was no whip in that choice. There was a call and an answer. The man laid his hand on each neck. He did not look like someone branding a tool. The water, shared afterwards, tasted better.",
    "The lines that are still spoken of with respect came from those five mares. A Saudi child learns those names the way she learns her family's names: slowly, and without mocking them. In the legend, the standard of the breed is not only a beautiful forehead. It is not giving up and not abandoning whoever calls, even with thirst shouting. The people, on the other side, do not abandon the horse. They give it water, shade and a place. On some nights the tent is shared. Leaving the asil outside, in the cold or in contempt, would be a great shame.",
    "Anyone who ill-treats the asil finds no excuse in the desert or at a neighbour's house. The sand has a short memory for liars and a long one for the cruel. The horse that resisted thirst teaches its owner to be no smaller than itself. Arabia, in this story, is measured in loyalty and in water given in time, not only in racing speed. If you call someone who trusts you, remember the five mares. They could have drunk first. They waited for the voice. That waiting is the true magic, and it needs no wand to be rare.",
  ],
  "ph:0": [
    "In the Tagalog creation myth, the world of the islands was still beginning. There was sea all around, sky above, and an enormous bamboo on the shore, closed like a secret. A bird, curious and patient, pecked at the stem once and again, until a crack opened. Two people came out from inside. One was Malakas, the strong. The other was Maganda, the beautiful. They did not come from a distant boat. They came from the stem, with the sea as witness and their feet still damp with shadow.",
    "Malakas looked at the islands and felt strong enough to walk, build and protect. Maganda looked at the same islands and saw beauty to care for, name and keep. They were not enemies and not each other's toys. They were the first pair, side by side, learning the ground. The broken bamboo became their first house. The bird settled nearby, like someone finishing their work. Without that beak, the stem would not have opened. Without the stem, in this version, the islands had no parents.",
    "The two walked along the coast, drank fresh water and listened to the wind in the leaves. Where there was hunger, they shared. Where there was fear of the dark, they lit a fire. In time, their people filled the beaches, and the archipelago found voices. Other Filipino peoples tell of other pairs and other origins, and we say this with respect. This tale is the one most taught in Tagalog schools. It does not erase the others. It opens a door: the door of a people who like to remember they were born from their own land.",
    "Malakas was not only muscle, and Maganda was not only a pretty face. Strength without care splits the bamboo too much. Beauty without strength cannot keep the house in a storm. Together, the two of them made the people. Children hear the stem cracking and imagine the happy fright of the first light. The sea around them is still a witness. When a wave hits the hollow trunk, some say you can still hear an echo. It is the sound of that crack, small and huge at once, close to the sand.",
    "Filipino children hear this myth as pride in where they come from. In this version, we did not come from outside. We came from the stem. Malakas is strength. Maganda is beauty. Together, the two are the people. If you see a tall bamboo beside the water, you can remember the bird that had the patience to peck. Here, the islands are born from an opening. Without the bird, there is no way out. Without the pair, there is no beginning. The sea stays all around, blue and wide, keeping the story without carrying it away.",
  ],
};
export default PART;
